gafi(){return"2 hole cards, 5 community cards: flop (3), turn and river. Your hand is the best 5 of any 7; you may play the board.\nSB and BB are posted left of the button. Preflop the player left of the BB acts first; after the flop, the first active player left of the button.\nHeads-up: the button posts the SB, acts first preflop and last after the flop.\nThe minimum raise equals the last raise. In No Limit the maximum is your whole stack.\nEqual hands are decided by kickers within 5 cards; identical hands split the pot."},
gafj(){return"An incomplete all-in (less than a min raise) does not reopen the betting for players who already acted: they may only call or fold.\nThe odd chip in a split goes to the first player left of the button.\nSuits are equal: a pot is never decided by suit.\nStraddle: classic is posted UTG for 2 BB and acts last preflop; Mississippi can be posted from any seat, usually the button. Whether it is allowed is a table decision.\n\u201cCards speak\u201d: the best hand wins at showdown even if it was called wrong. Agree on this up front in home games."},
gafp(){return"4\u20137 hole cards (Omaha 4/5/6/7), 5 community cards.\nYour hand uses exactly 2 hole cards and exactly 3 board cards. Four suited cards on the board are not a flush unless you hold two of that suit.\nUsually Pot Limit (PL): the maximum bet is the pot size including your call.\nBlinds and action order are the same as in Hold'em."},
gafq(){return"Pot-sized raise: first call, then raise by the size of the resulting pot.\nIn 6\u20137 card Omaha with a full table the deck may run out \u2014 agree beforehand whether to finish the board from the muck.\nAt showdown, show all hole cards, not just the two you use."},
gafg(){return"The pot is split: high (normal hand) and low.\nLow is 5 different cards 8 or lower, ace low; straights and flushes don't hurt it. The best low is A-2-3-4-5.\nHigh and low each use 2 hole cards and 3 board cards \u2014 they can be different cards.\nIf there is no low (fewer than three different cards 8 or lower on the board), high takes the whole pot."},
gafh(){return"Quartering: if two players split the low, each gets \xbc of the pot.\nThe odd chip between high and low goes to high.\nLows are compared from the top card down: 7-5-4-3-2 beats 7-6-3-2-A."},
gafr(){return"3 hole cards. After the flop, before betting on it, everyone discards one card; then it plays like Hold'em.\nDiscarded cards are dead and never return to the board."},
gafs(){return"Discard variants: Crazy Pineapple discards after the flop betting, Lazy Pineapple before showdown. Agree which one you play.\nWith up to 9 players there are enough cards (27 dealt)."},
gafm(){return"13 cards in 3 rows: top 3, middle 5, bottom 5. Bottom must not be weaker than middle, middle not weaker than top \u2014 otherwise it's a foul.\nScore against each opponent separately: +1 per row won, +3 for a scoop (all three rows), plus the royalty difference.\nFoul: you lose every row (\u22126 with the scoop) and your royalties.\nPineapple OFC: first 5 cards, then 3 at a time with one discarded.\nFantasyland: queens or better on top without fouling \u2014 next hand you get all cards at once."},
gafn(){return"Royalties and Fantasyland terms vary. Progressive: QQ \u2014 14 cards, KK \u2014 15, AA \u2014 16, trips \u2014 17.\nStaying in Fantasyland usually needs trips on top, a full house in the middle or quads on the bottom.\nEqual hands in a row are compared by kickers; fully equal is a tie on that row."},
gafw(){return"36-card deck (6\u2013A), 2 hole cards, the hand plays like Hold'em.\nThe ace is high and low: A-6-7-8-9 is the lowest straight.\nOften played with antes and a button blind instead of SB and BB \u2014 check the table format.\nHand rankings follow one of three variants (switch above)."},
gafx(){return"Trips vs straight depends on the table: in \u201cstandard\u201d the straight wins, in \u201ctrips > straight\u201d it's the other way round. Agree before playing.\nA flush beats a full house almost everywhere; \u201cclassic\u201d, where the full house wins, is rare.\nOdds differ: AA vs a random hand is about 77%, not 85% as in Hold'em."},
